Offset Pagination vs Cursor Pagination (Keyset)

────────────────────────────────────────
1️⃣ Offset Pagination

📌 What it is

Uses LIMIT + OFFSET in SQL.

SELECT *
FROM users
ORDER BY id
LIMIT 10 OFFSET 20;

Means: skip first 20 rows, return next 10.

📌 How it works

UI-style 1-based page numbers:

OFFSET = (pageNumber - 1) * pageSize
page 3, size 10 → OFFSET = 20

Spring Data Pageable is 0-based:
page 0 → first page (OFFSET 0)
page 2 → third page (OFFSET 20)
PageRequest.of(page, size) → same OFFSET idea.

📌 Example

Page 1: LIMIT 10 OFFSET 0
Page 2: LIMIT 10 OFFSET 10
Page 3: LIMIT 10 OFFSET 20

📌 Advantages

✅ Simple
✅ Easy “go to page 50”
✅ Good for admin tables / small-medium data

📌 Problems

❌ Slow for deep pages / large data
   Deep OFFSET often forces the DB to walk/skip many rows before returning the page.

❌ Unstable if rows insert/delete while paging
   Can see duplicates or miss rows between page requests.

Spring example: Specification + Page / PageRequest (OFFSET).

────────────────────────────────────────
2️⃣ Cursor Pagination (also called Keyset Pagination)

📌 What it is

No OFFSET. Resume from a cursor = last seen sort-key value(s).

SELECT *
FROM users
WHERE id > 20
ORDER BY id
LIMIT 10;

`20` = last `id` from previous page (the cursor).

📌 How it works

Page 1:
SELECT * FROM users ORDER BY id LIMIT 10;
→ remember last returned id (e.g. 15) — not “always page*size”

Page 2:
SELECT * FROM users WHERE id > 15 ORDER BY id LIMIT 10;

Important: cursor = actual last value from previous result, not a calculated OFFSET.

📌 Composite cursor (real world)

If you sort by created_at (not unique), add a unique tie-break (usually id):

WHERE (created_at > :ts)
   OR (created_at = :ts AND id > :id)
ORDER BY created_at, id
LIMIT 10

📌 Advantages

✅ Fast on large data (index-friendly seek)
✅ No deep OFFSET skip cost
✅ More stable with inserts (continues after last seen key)

📌 Limitations

❌ Cannot jump directly to “page 50”
❌ Needs stable ORDER BY + indexed cursor columns
❌ Slightly more complex (encode/pass cursor)
❌ Sort keys should be non-null for reliable keyset

Spring example: manual `id > :cursor` query, or ScrollPosition + Window (keyset).

────────────────────────────────────────
🔥 Comparison

| Feature              | Offset Pagination      | Cursor / Keyset        |
| -------------------- | ---------------------- | ---------------------- |
| Large data           | Slow for deep pages    | Fast                   |
| Jump to page N       | Yes                    | No (next/prev only)    |
| Inserts while paging | Can dup/miss           | More consistent        |
| Implementation       | Easy                   | Medium                 |
| Typical SQL          | LIMIT/OFFSET           | WHERE key > last LIMIT |

────────────────────────────────────────
🎯 When to use what

Use OFFSET when:
- Small/medium dataset
- Admin panel with page numbers
- Need “page 50” jump

Use CURSOR / KEYSET when:
- Large dataset
- Infinite scroll / feeds / mobile APIs
- High throughput list endpoints

Examples: Instagram/Twitter-style feeds → cursor  
Admin dashboards → offset

────────────────────────────────────────
Vs Spring Data ScrollPosition

Cursor/keyset = the technique.
ScrollPosition.keyset() + Window = Spring Data’s built-in way to do keyset scrolling.
Manual `WHERE id > ?` = same idea, full SQL control.

────────────────────────────────────────
🚀 Interview Answer

Offset pagination uses LIMIT/OFFSET — simple and allows jumping to any page,
but deep pages get slow and concurrent inserts can cause duplicates/missed rows.

Cursor (keyset) pagination uses a reference like id > lastSeen with ORDER BY + LIMIT.
It is faster and more consistent for large/infinite-scroll APIs, but you cannot jump to an arbitrary page number.
